// Fixture: emit_all_domains_minimal
// Rule under test: §8.5 / §14.7 -- --emit-all-domains flag, all four
// export targets.
//
// Deliberately minimal and isolated: one domain with a declared width
// that is never referenced by any field in any exported type. The
// exported type (Item, with one plain u8 field) exists only to give
// the exporter something concrete to export -- the gap under test is
// about Rarity, not about Item.
//
// Dual-purpose across two channels (§8.5 / §14.7 are two different
// mechanisms for the same underlying need):
//   - On 6502, Z80, 68000: Rarity's entire domain entry (member index
//     constants, jump table on 6502, dispatch on 6502) is suppressed
//     by default. --emit-all-domains bypasses the per-target
//     `if domain not in used: continue` guard and emits the same
//     constant-table form a referenced domain of the same shape already
//     produces.
//   - On C++: Rarity's primary `enum class Rarity : uint64_t` is always
//     emitted (every domain's logical-ID enum is unconditional on C++).
//     Only the `_Indexed` companion (`Rarity_Indexed : uint8_t`) is
//     gated -- it only appears when something uses `@Rarity` in the
//     compiled data, or when --emit-all-domains is on.
// This distinction is confirmed directly against each exporter's code
// and a real fixture; §8.5 states it explicitly.
//
// Values chosen to be obviously recognizable in output: common=0,
// rare=1, epic=2, all fitting in u8.

identifier Rarity u8
	common = "Common tier"
	rare   = "Rare tier"
	epic   = "Epic tier"

// Item has no Rarity field. This is the entire point of the fixture.
define Item
	power = u8

Item BasicSword
	power = 10
